Dear Roksana and the VoxelMatters team,

thank you for the detailed Solidon3D article you published on 22 August:
https://www.voxelmatters.com/solidon3d-turns-a-downloaded-stl-into-a-part-that-fits-without-cad/

You already covered the four workflows, operation history, print checks,
offline use, demo terms and pricing. Rather than repeat that introduction, I
would like to offer only the concrete 0.2.2 follow-up.

The main additions beyond the workflow described in your article are:

* users can turn selected history steps into reusable parametric components,
  with named fields, a preview and a tested range, without executable code;
* typed sketch dimensions remain as constraints, while closed contours can be
  pulled into solids or pockets with a live dimensional preview;
* matching printable screws and nuts, standards-based bearing seats,
  countersinks and washers have been added;
* each physical filament spool can carry temperature, cooling, retraction and
  material data into the correct 3MF slot and external slicer;
* Linux now has an AppImage in addition to Flatpak, with more reliable local
  slicer discovery and update handling.

Version 0.2.2 alone documents 40 curated user-facing changes. The complete
history since the first 0.1 demo now contains 220.

Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html
Download: https://solidon3d.de/en/

If a short update is useful, I can provide new images and one sample project
showing a custom component through to a material-aware 3MF handoff.

